From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] color: a virtual white equal to the panel white keeps scanout

calcScanoutLinearScale() refuses whenever a virtual white point is set, on the
grounds that a chromatic adaptation is a real matrix and the DPU cannot carry
one. That is right for an adaptation between two DIFFERENT white points and
wrong for the case that actually ships: the Steam client publishes the panel's
own white whenever the colour temperature slider sits at neutral, which is where
it sits out of the box, and adapting a white point to itself is the identity --
a diagonal, and therefore expressible.

So the default configuration composited every frame in order to apply a
transform that does nothing, surrendering exactly what DPU inline rotation and
0014 were built to keep.

CAUGHT ON HARDWARE, Pocket ACE, and it only became reachable with 0016: before
that the arm64 client's y arrived as a zero overread and the gate never fired,
so this was latent in 0014 from the start and would equally have fired for any
client that packs the property correctly. Night mode at 0.5/0.95 with the client's
own virtual white left the CRTC's CTM at identity; clearing the virtual white
alone brought back diag(1.0, 0.72315, 0.52500), which is hsv_to_rgb(25deg, 0.475,
1.0) = (1.0, 0.72292, 0.525) to 2e-4.

THE TOLERANCE IS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN. The panel white and the client's neutral
are not bit-identical -- on a board with no display profile the panel white is
the synthetic EDID's 10-bit quantised D65, 0.3125000 against the client's
0.3127789, a gap of 2.8e-4 -- so an exact comparison would not fire. One notch
of the slider moves x by 7.4e-3, twenty-six times that gap. 0.001 sits 3.6x above
the gap and 7.4x below a notch, so it can neither miss neutral nor swallow the
slider's first step. Both bounds are pinned in a test using the measured values.

A real adjustment still composites, and that remains correct rather than a
compromise: an adaptation between different white points is not diagonal in the
panel's RGB space and genuinely cannot go in the CTM.

TESTS DROPPED 2026-10-25 (gamescope 3.17.0): the two cases this added to
tests/test_drm_color_pipeline.cpp went with that file (see 0014). The PKGBUILD
builds with -Denable_tests=false, so they never compiled in our build. The
measured bounds above are the record; nothing re-checks them now.

diff --git a/src/color_helpers.cpp b/src/color_helpers.cpp
index 0aba314..bddf2b5 100644
--- a/src/color_helpers.cpp
+++ b/src/color_helpers.cpp
@@ -896,11 +896,32 @@ std::optional<glm::vec3> calcScanoutLinearScale(
     // carry this even approximately.
     (void)mapping;
     (void)source;
-    (void)dest;
 
-    // The virtual white point adaptation is a real matrix; we only handle its
-    // disabled case. Same condition calcColorTransform() uses to skip it.
-    if ( destVirtualWhite.x > 0.01f && destVirtualWhite.y > 0.01f )
+    // The virtual white point adaptation is a real matrix, so we only handle the cases
+    // where it is not actually a transform. There are two, and the second one is the
+    // whole shipped default.
+    //
+    // The first is an UNSET white point -- the same condition calcColorTransform() uses
+    // to skip the adaptation entirely.
+    //
+    // The second is a white point equal to THE PANEL'S OWN, which is the identity
+    // adaptation, and the identity is a diagonal, so it is expressible. This is not an
+    // exotic case: the Steam client publishes the panel's white whenever the colour
+    // temperature slider sits at neutral, which is where it sits out of the box. Without
+    // this clause every frame of the default configuration composites in order to apply
+    // a transform that does nothing -- surrendering exactly what inline rotation and this
+    // function were built to keep. Measured on Pocket ACE, which is where it was caught:
+    // the client sends x = 0.3127789 against a synthetic-EDID panel white of 0.3125000,
+    // a gap of 2.8e-4, while ONE notch of the slider moves x by 7.4e-3. The tolerance
+    // below sits 3.6x above the gap and 7.4x below a notch, so it can neither miss
+    // neutral nor swallow a real adjustment.
+    constexpr float k_flVirtualWhiteNeutralEpsilon = 0.001f;
+    const bool bVirtualWhiteSet =
+        destVirtualWhite.x > 0.01f && destVirtualWhite.y > 0.01f;
+    const bool bVirtualWhiteIsPanelWhite =
+        std::fabs( destVirtualWhite.x - dest.white.x ) < k_flVirtualWhiteNeutralEpsilon &&
+        std::fabs( destVirtualWhite.y - dest.white.y ) < k_flVirtualWhiteNeutralEpsilon;
+    if ( bVirtualWhiteSet && !bVirtualWhiteIsPanelWhite )
         return std::nullopt;
 
     (void)eMethod;
